    <!-- Watch Out For -->
    <section class="exam-section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #fff3cd 0%, #ffe5a0 100%); border-left-color: #ffc107;">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation section-icon" style="color: #856404;"></i> Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>BST Search:</strong> Assuming a BST is always $O(\log n)$; students must see that a "skewed" tree performs like a linked list ($O(n)$).</li>
            <li><strong>Linked List Pointers:</strong> Forgetting to update the "Tail" or "Prev" pointers during deletion, leading to memory leaks or broken chains.</li>
            <li><strong>Hashing:</strong> Believing a "perfect" hash function eliminates the need for collision handling.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Big Ideas -->
    <section class="content-section" style="border-top-color: var(--accent-color);">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb section-icon"></i> Big Ideas to Consider</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Models vs Reality:</strong> ADTs are mathematical models. To what extent does the "purity" of a model like a BST mask the messy physical reality of memory fragmentation?</li>
                <li><strong>Categorisation:</strong> How does the way we "hash" and categorise data change the way we retrieve and value that knowledge?</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

